{"title":"Teach the Intern","markdown":{"headingText":"Teach the Intern","headingAttr":{"id":"sec-teach-the-intern","classes":[],"keyvalue":[]},"containsRefs":false,"markdown":"\n> Every chat that starts from zero wastes everything you already learned.\n\n**Lab companion:** [Lab 6, Teach the Intern](https://michael-borck.github.io/programming-labs/lab-06-teach-the-intern/) — this chapter is the reasoning; the lab is the doing.\n\n## The Concept First\n\nYour department has one crippling trait left: amnesia. Every fresh conversation, the agent arrives knowing everything about software and nothing about your project — your standards, your decisions, the client's quirks, the three things you decided were out of scope last Tuesday. So you re-explain. And the re-explanations drift, because explanations given casually are never quite the same twice.\n\nThe fix is embarrassingly physical: **a folder of markdown files in the repository**. Instructions the agent reads at the start of every session. Decisions recorded as they are made. Preferences the agent should stop guessing about. This is the context folder — with `AGENTS.md` as its front door — and it is the single highest-leverage artefact in AI-era work.\n\nTwo reasons it matters more than any clever prompt. First, it converts your private knowledge into a company asset: the department stops depending on your presence. Second, it is the foundation of the skill-folder architecture in Chapter 12 — the filing cabinet that lets one agent become a whole department. You cannot organise skills you have not first written down.\n\n## Understanding Through the New Barista\n\nA new barista asks the regulars their orders every morning for a month. Sweet, but slow — and the day the barista is replaced, all that learning walks out. The good cafe writes the orders down.\n\nYour context folder is the orders notebook. The test of every entry: *could a replacement worker do it right from the file, without asking?* If a note only makes sense to you, it is a memory aid. If it works for a stranger, it is a **system**.\n\n## Discovering the Context Folder with Your AI Partner\n\n### Exploration 1: Audit the Re-explanations\n\nThink back over your last three agent conversations. What did you explain more than once?\n\n```text\nHere are the last three briefs I gave you in separate conversations.\nIdentify everything I re-explained — every instruction, preference,\nor decision that appeared in more than one brief. List them as\ncandidates for a standing instructions file.\n```\n\nThat list is your context folder's first draft. Repetition is the signal: anything explained twice belongs in a file.\n\n### Exploration 2: The Front Door\n\nThe file `AGENTS.md` at the repository root is read by agents before they start work. Fill it with what a replacement needs:\n\n```markdown\n# Project: Sunrise Cafe page\n\n## What this is\nA one-page site for a small cafe. Plain HTML/CSS, no frameworks,\ndeliberately.\n\n## Standards\n- One file per concern; no build tools\n- Australian English spelling\n- Mobile-first: the page must work at 360px width\n- All text content comes from content.md, never hard-coded\n\n## Decisions\n- No contact form: the client wants phone calls (decided 2026-09-10)\n- Menu items live in a list at the top of index.html, so the client\n  can edit them without help\n\n## Never\n- No external fonts or scripts (client is on slow rural internet)\n- Do not add pages without a spec\n```\n\n### Exploration 3: Watch the Difference\n\nStart a fresh conversation. Ask the agent to add a new menu item — nothing else. Then ask it how it knew the spelling convention, the mobile rule, or the no-new-pages decision. Its answer should cite the file. If it guessed anyway, your AGENTS.md was not read — which happens when the file is vague, buried, or contradicts itself. Fix the file, not the agent.\n\n## From Concept to Code\n\nThe context folder grows one file at a time, each with one job:\n\n```text\nproject/\n├── AGENTS.md          # Front door: standards, decisions, never-list\n├── SPEC.md            # What is being built and what done means\n├── DECISIONS.md       # The decision log: what, why, when\n├── CRITIQUE.md        # What you would still change, and why\n└── content.md         # The client's actual words and prices\n```\n\nThe discipline that keeps it alive: **the moment a decision is made, it goes in the file.** Not after the project. The moment. Decisions recorded later are decisions reconstructed from a chat transcript — which is archaeology, not management.\n\n::: {.callout-important title=\"Contradictions are poison\"}\nAn agent reading \"always ask before deleting\" in one file and \"clean up old files automatically\" in another will pick one and run with it — silently. When the department does something inexplicable, the first place to look is a contradiction between standing documents. One topic, one file, one ruling.\n:::\n\n## Business Connection\n\nThe context folder is the difference between *your* AI advantage and an AI advantage the business owns. Everything in your head is a single point of failure with a notice period. Everything in the folder survives your holiday, your resignation, and the migration to next year's model.\n\nIt is also the cheapest onboarding ever invented. A new employee, a new contractor, a new agent version — all of them start by reading the same four files. The business that writes its decisions down is the business that can change hands.\n\n## Practice Exercises\n\n**Exercise 7.1 (Level 1).** Create AGENTS.md with three sections: What this is, Standards, Never. Ten lines is enough.\n\n**Exercise 7.2 (Level 2).** Start a fresh agent conversation and ask for a change without mentioning the file. Did the agent read it? How do you know?\n\n**Exercise 7.3 (Level 3).** Add DECISIONS.md. Backfill the last three decisions you made on this project, each with its date and reason.\n\n**Exercise 7.4 (Level 4).** Deliberately introduce a contradiction between two files, then ask the agent to perform the affected task. Observe which ruling it followed. Document the behaviour.\n\n**Exercise 7.5 (Level 5).** Write the onboarding set for a business process you run outside software: what would a folder need to contain for a replacement to run your Tuesday?\n\n## Chapter Summary\n\n- Re-explaining is the tax; the context folder abolishes it\n- AGENTS.md is the front door: standards, decisions, the never-list\n- One topic, one file, one ruling — contradictions poison the department\n- Decisions go in the file the moment they are made, not later\n\n## Reflection\n\nBefore moving on, ensure you:\n\n- [ ] Have AGENTS.md in the repository, actually read by the agent\n- [ ] Backfilled your decisions with reasons\n- [ ] Tested the folder with a fresh conversation\n- [ ] Fixed at least one contradiction or vagueness the test exposed\n\n## Your Learning Journal\n\n1. What instruction, repeated three times, is finally in a file?\n2. What decision did you record — and what reason surprised you when you wrote it down?\n3. What process in your life would collapse without its notebook?\n\n## Next Steps\n\nBriefed, recorded, and taught. The next chapter is the trust exercise: the department sounds certain — so you make it show you the evidence.\n","srcMarkdownNoYaml":""},"formats":{"html":{"identifier":{"display-name":"HTML","target-format":"html","base-format":"html"},"execute":{"fig-width":7,"fig-height":5,"fig-format":"retina","fig-dpi":96,"df-print":"default","error":false,"eval":true,"cache":null,"freeze":false,"echo":true,"output":true,"warning":false,"include":true,"keep-md":false,"keep-ipynb":false,"ipynb":null,"enabled":null,"daemon":null,"daemon-restart":false,"debug":false,"ipynb-filters":[],"ipynb-shell-interactivity":null,"plotly-connected":true,"engine":"markdown"},"render":{"keep-tex":false,"keep-typ":false,"keep-source":false,"keep-hidden":false,"prefer-html":false,"output-divs":true,"output-ext":"htm